This is the most interesting part:
<ul data-sourcepos=3D"44:9-49:76">
<li data-sourcepos=3D"44:9-44:390"><code>n</code>: This calculates the <em>=
current total number of records</em> that the <code>NameManager</code> is a=
ware of. It's the sum of records loaded from the DB at the start (<code>sel=
f.all_person_records.len()</code>) and the new records <em>just added in th=
is run</em> (<code>added_ids_this_run.len()</code>). This is a <strong>smar=
t way to track progress based on the evolving dataset size</strong>, rather=
 than just the input loop index <code>i</code>.</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"45:9-45:84"><code>last</code>: Gets the maximum value=
 from the <code>intervals</code> array (which is 5000).</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"46:9-48:369"><strong>Training Condition:</strong>
<ul data-sourcepos=3D"47:13-48:369">
<li data-sourcepos=3D"47:13-47:171"><code>intervals.contains(&amp;n)</code>=
: Trains if the current total number of records <code>n</code> matches one =
of the predefined fixed points (100, 200, 500, 1000, 2000, 5000).</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"48:13-48:369"><code>n &gt; last &amp;&amp; n % last =
=3D=3D 0</code>: This handles training <em>after</em> the last fixed interv=
al. If <code>n</code> exceeds the largest fixed interval (5000) and <code>n=
</code> is a multiple of that largest interval (e.g., 6000, 7000, 10000, et=
c.), it will trigger another training. This ensures continuous training for=
 very large datasets beyond the explicitly defined fixed points.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"49:9-49:76"><code>name_manager.train_ml_model()?;</co=
de>: Executes the ML model training.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"50:5-55:80"><strong>Matching Logic:</strong>
<ul data-sourcepos=3D"51:9-55:80">
<li data-sourcepos=3D"51:9-51:140"><code>name_manager.find_matches(&amp;nam=
e_input)</code>: Attempts to find matches for the <code>name_input</code> i=
n the <code>NameManager</code>'s in-memory data.</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"52:9-55:80">The <code>match</code> statement then eva=
luates the <code>MatchResult</code>:
<ul data-sourcepos=3D"53:13-55:80">
<li data-sourcepos=3D"53:13-53:51"><code>ExactMatch</code>: No new record i=
s added.</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"54:13-54:214"><code>HighConfidenceMatch</code>: If co=
nfidence is <code>&gt;=3D 0.95</code>, no new record is added. Otherwise (<=
code>&lt; 0.95</code>), <code>should_add_new</code> is set to <code>true</c=
ode>. This implies a threshold for what constitutes a "definite" match.</li=
>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"55:13-55:80"><code>PossibleMatches</code> or <code>No=
Match</code>: <code>should_add_new</code> is always <code>true</code>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"56:5-61:0"><strong>Record Creation and Buffering:</st=
rong>
<ul data-sourcepos=3D"57:9-61:0">
<li data-sourcepos=3D"57:9-58:286">If <code>should_add_new</code> is <code>=
true</code>, a new <code>PersonRecord</code> is created using <code>name_ma=
nager.create_person_record(&amp;name_input, provided_id)</code>.
<ul data-sourcepos=3D"58:13-58:286">
<li data-sourcepos=3D"58:13-58:286"><strong>Crucial Assumption</strong>: <c=
ode>name_manager.create_person_record</code> must internally handle the log=
ic of using <code>provided_id</code> if it's <code>Some</code>, or generati=
ng a new ID if it's <code>None</code>, and updating <code>name_manager</cod=
e>'s internal max ID tracking. This is consistent with previous discussions=
.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"59:9-59:264">The new record is then immediately added=
 to the <code>name_manager</code>'s in-memory store (<code>name_manager.add=
_person_record(new_record.clone())?</code>). This ensures that subsequent n=
ames in the <em>same batch</em> can potentially find matches against this n=
ewly added record.</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"60:9-61:0">The <code>new_record</code> is also pushed=
 to <code>new_person_records_to_add_buffer</code> and its ID to <code>added=
_ids_this_run</code>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
