<p data-sourcepos=3D"52:9-52:72">This is the most sophisticated part of the=
 ML training strategy:</p>
<ul data-sourcepos=3D"53:9-59:0">
<li data-sourcepos=3D"53:9-53:478"><strong><code>n</code> calculation</stro=
ng>: <code>n</code> represents the <em>current total number of names that t=
he <code>NameManager</code> is aware of and actively uses for matching</em>=
. It combines the names loaded from the database at the start (<code>self.a=
ll_person_records.len()</code>) with the names just added during <em>this s=
pecific <code>process_names</code> run</em> (<code>added_ids_this_run.len()=
</code>). This is a <strong>highly effective way to base training on the gr=
owing effective dataset size, not just the index of the current input name<=
/strong>.</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"54:9-54:103"><strong><code>last</code></strong>: Corr=
ectly retrieves the maximum value from the <code>intervals</code> array (wh=
ich is 5000).</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"55:9-57:383"><strong>Training Condition</strong>:
<ul data-sourcepos=3D"56:13-57:383">
<li data-sourcepos=3D"56:13-56:232"><code>intervals.contains(&amp;n)</code>=
: This triggers training precisely when the current total number of names (=
<code>n</code>) matches one of your predefined fixed points (100, 200, 500,=
 1000, 2000, 5000). This provides granular control.</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"57:13-57:383"><code>n &gt; last &amp;&amp; n % last =
=3D=3D 0</code>: This is a clever extension. If <code>n</code> exceeds the =
largest fixed interval (5000), it will trigger additional training sessions=
 every time <code>n</code> is a multiple of that largest interval (e.g., at=
 6000, 7000, 10000, 15000, etc.). This ensures the model continues to be up=
dated for very large datasets that go beyond your initial fixed points.</li=
>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"58:9-59:0"><strong><code>name_manager.train_ml_model(=
)?;</code></strong>: This is where the actual ML model training happens.</l=
i>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"60:5-66:0">
<p data-sourcepos=3D"60:7-60:25"><strong>Matching Logic:</strong></p>
<ul data-sourcepos=3D"61:9-66:0">
<li data-sourcepos=3D"61:9-61:218"><code>name_manager.find_matches(&amp;nam=
e_input)</code>: This is the core deduplication and similarity check. It co=
mpares the current <code>name_input</code> against the names already presen=
t in the <code>NameManager</code>'s in-memory data.</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"62:9-66:0">The subsequent <code>match</code> statemen=
t evaluates the <code>MatchResult</code>:
<ul data-sourcepos=3D"63:13-66:0">
<li data-sourcepos=3D"63:13-63:96"><strong><code>ExactMatch(_)</code></stro=
ng>: If an exact match is found, <code>should_add_new</code> remains <code>=
false</code>.</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"64:13-64:411"><strong><code>HighConfidenceMatch(match=
es)</code></strong>: If the highest confidence match is <code>&gt;=3D 0.95<=
/code>, it's considered a definitive match, and <code>should_add_new</code>=
 is <code>false</code>. If the confidence is <code>&lt; 0.95</code>, it's n=
ot a strong enough match to prevent a new addition, so <code>should_add_new=
</code> becomes <code>true</code>. This implies a configurable threshold fo=
r what constitutes a "definite" match versus a "possible but still new" ent=
ry.</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"65:13-66:0"><strong><code>PossibleMatches(_) | NoMatc=
h</code></strong>: In these cases, <code>should_add_new</code> is always <c=
ode>true</code>, meaning a new record will likely be created.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"67:5-71:0">
<p data-sourcepos=3D"67:7-67:49"><strong>Record Creation and In-Memory Addi=
tion:</strong></p>
<ul data-sourcepos=3D"68:9-71:0">
<li data-sourcepos=3D"68:9-68:341">If <code>should_add_new</code> is <code>=
true</code>, a new <code>PersonRecord</code> is created using <code>name_ma=
nager.create_person_record(&amp;name_input, provided_id)</code>. <strong>It=
's crucial that this method correctly handles <code>provided_id</code> (usi=
ng it if <code>Some</code>, generating a new one if <code>None</code>) and =
updates <code>NameManager</code>'s internal state to track the highest assi=
gned ID.</strong></li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"69:9-69:361">The newly created <code>new_record</code=
> is immediately added to the <code>name_manager</code>'s in-memory store (=
<code>name_manager.add_person_record(new_record.clone())?</code>). This is =
a key optimization: <strong>future names processed in the <em>same batch</e=
m> will be able to find matches against this newly added record</strong>, i=
mproving the accuracy of <code>find_matches</code> as the batch progresses.=
</li>
<li data-sourcepos=3D"70:9-71:0">The record is also pushed to <code>new_per=
son_records_to_add_buffer</code> for later batch saving and its ID is added=
 to <code>added_ids_this_run</code>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
